[ ] 20 Add an on-demand poll mechanism for Tasmota devices that piggybacks on
       the new MQTT sink: a node (or a worksheet recipe documented in the help)
       that publishes "cmnd/tasmotas/Status 5" to the broker and listens on
       "stat/+/STATUS5" through an MQTT source, then surfaces each device's
       IPAddress / Hostname / Mac from the StatusNET reply. The result is a
       discovery feed that can be wired into the HTTPS Client (or a future plain
       HTTP client) to drive per-device REST calls without any external scan.

[x] 22 Implement a tasmota concentrator. Here is how a discovery message can be
       sent to a tasmota device:
       mosquitto_pub -h mqtt.homelab.local -p 1883 -t 'cmnd/sonoff19/Status' -m '5'

       Why this needs to be per-device (verified live 2026-06-06 against the
       homelab broker while building PnTasmotaStatusRequest, the manual
       single-shot building block): a command published to the "tasmotas" GROUP
       topic (cmnd/tasmotas/Status) is acted on by every device but Tasmota
       deliberately suppresses the MQTT reply, so you get ZERO stat/+/STATUS
       responses back. A request to a SINGLE device (cmnd/sonoff47/Status 0)
       returns the full STATUS..STATUS11 set. MQTT wildcards (#, +) are
       subscribe-only and illegal in a publish topic; "*" is a literal, not a
       wildcard. Conclusion: to read info back from EVERY device you must send
       one request per device -- there is no broadcast-and-collect shortcut. The
       concentrator automates that loop.

       Design: two cooperating nodes (both core-only, plugins/tasmota tier),
       used alongside the existing PnTasmotaStatusRequest.

       [x] 22.1 Prober node (PnTasmotaProbe). DONE. Input wires from an MQTT
           Source on the broker; output wires to an MQTT Sink. Passively watches
           inbound traffic for Tasmota device names (parses the <device> segment
           out of tele/<device>/..., stat/<device>/..., cmnd/<device>/...; other
           prefixes and the "tasmotas" group topic are ignored), keeps a live
           set of known devices, and emits per-device discovery requests on its
           output (cmnd/<device>/Status <n>, same shape as
           PnTasmotaStatusRequest) so the Sink publishes them. Rate-limit:
           newly-seen device probed promptly, an already-known one never
           re-probed more often than the configurable `interval` (seconds,
           default 300; 0 = every message). `request` property picks the Status
           section (shared PnTasmotaStatusKind enum, lifted from status-request
           into pn-tasmota-common). fa-binoculars icon, violet body. Help page +
           8-case unit test (tests/unit/test-pn-tasmota-probe.c) added.

       [x] 22.2 Concentrator / collector node (PnTasmotaConcentrator). DONE.
           Input wires from the MQTT Source (the stat/<device>/STATUS* replies,
           optionally also tele/.../STATE|SENSOR via the merge-telemetry prop).
           Accumulates a per-device record, top-level-merging each STATUS /
           STATUS5 / STATUS11 fragment into one object keyed by device. Emit
           policy: when an accepted message arrives AND >= `interval` seconds
           (default 2, debounce; 0 = every message) have elapsed since the last
           emit, sends ONE combined message with data.devices = { <device>:
           {..merged..}, ... } + data.value (device count) to a downstream Debug
           / table / HTTP view. Holds state across messages; optional `timeout`
           (default 0 = never) drops devices gone silent. fa-database icon,
           sea-green body. Help page + 10-case unit test
           (tests/unit/test-pn-tasmota-concentrator.c) added.

       [x] 22.3 Wiring recipe + example worksheet. DONE.
           examples/home-automation/tasmota-discovery.json wires MQTT Source ->
           Tasmota Probe -> MQTT Sink and MQTT Source -> Tasmota Concentrator ->
           Debug (registered in examples/Makefile.am; loads clean under
           pipnode-run). Help pages for both nodes already shipped with
           22.1/22.2 and document the group-topic no-reply gotcha
           (PnTasmotaProbe.html "Why per-device, not one broadcast").

       [x] 22.4 Keep prober and concentrator as SEPARATE nodes. DONE -- they are
           two independently-wireable core nodes (PnTasmotaProbe,
           PnTasmotaConcentrator) and the tasmota-discovery.json example
           composes them into the full discover-poll-collect-present loop rather
           than fusing them.

       Relation to item (20): (20) is the same idea at recipe level (publish
       Status 5, listen on stat/+/STATUS5, surface IPAddress/Hostname/Mac for
       REST follow-up); (22) is the node-based, auto-discovering, all-sections
       generalisation -- folding (20)'s StatusNET-for-REST use case in here is
       reasonable.

