[x] 48 A proper, fast test/example for the error-bar Graph. Today the only way
       to see the Graph node's "Error bars" draw-style is to leave a live feed
       running for hours: the node bins SCALAR values by wall-clock arrival time
       (g_get_monotonic_time), one sample per bin, so the box-and-whisker glyphs
       only fill in as real time passes. That also made the single-sample case
       look like scattered dashes -- just fixed by extending each whisker
       halfway toward its neighbouring buckets' means (lib/pn-graph-gui.c
       collect_error_bars), but the fix can only be eyeballed against a slow
       live feed. We want a deterministic, instant way to drive the same
       rendering from a vector so the look can be checked from an example
       worksheet and a headless test.

         [x] 48.1 New graph node that renders exactly like PnGraph but ingests
               its data the way PnOscilloscope does -- a VECTOR on data.value is
               taken as the whole dataset in one message
               (pn_message_resolve_vector, the snapshot path), instead of
               time-binned scalar arrivals. Feed it a Ramp (PN_TYPE_RAMP emits a
               $pnvector on data.value) and the graph fills instantly. Sibling
               node mirroring PnXYGraph/PnOscilloscope: core lib/pn-<name>.c/.h
               + gui companion lib/pn-<name>-gui.c/.h, registered in
               pn-node-factory.c (PN_TYPE) and pn-gui.c (gui_install), four
               entries in lib/Makefile.am, plus a help page. REUSE the existing
               PnGraphBin model + collect_error_bars + draw_* path so every
               draw-style (Lines/Points/Bars/Error bars/2D/3D) works unchanged
               -- the node only swaps the receive() front-end (vector -> bins)
               for PnGraph's (scalar -> time bin). DECIDED: a new SIBLING node
               (PnGraph left untouched). DONE: name = PnPlot ("Plot",
               fa-line-chart, Sinks, orange). Core lib/pn-plot.c/.h + gui
               lib/pn-plot-gui.c/.h; the six reusable PnGraph draw/collect
               helpers were exposed via a new pn-graph-draw.h (un-static'd +
               pn_graph_* prefixed in pn-graph-gui.c, zero logic change) and the
               PnPlot painter calls them with a bucket-indexed X axis (cur_epoch
               = M-1, oldest_valid = 0) instead of PnGraph's wall-clock epoch,
               so Error-bars (incl. the neighbour-reach whisker fix),
               Lines/Points/Bars and the Distribution view all render
               identically. Registered + Makefile + PnPlot.html help all wired;
               builds clean, 96 C unit tests green.
         [x] 48.2 Vector -> bucket mapping: DECIDED -- distribute the N vector
               elements across M consecutive X-buckets (M = the node's bin-count
               property), each bucket aggregating its run into real
               mean/sd/min/max so the box-and-whisker glyphs are genuinely fat.
               Setting M near N collapses to ~one sample/bucket and re-exercises
               the new neighbour-reaching whisker path, so both looks are
               reachable from the one node by changing the bin count. DONE in
               pn-plot.c rebucket(): bucket b owns elements [b*N/M,(b+1)*N/M),
               epoch == bucket index; the held vector is re-binned live on an
               x-buckets change. Verified headless: vector 0..11 / M=4 -> means
               1,4,7,10 (min/max exact); M=12 -> 12 single-sample buckets.
               Default M = 16 (fat buckets) since the dataset arrives whole, not
               over time.
         [x] 48.3 Example worksheet under examples/ (Ramp [+ jitter source] ->
               new graph in Error-bars style), committed so the look is
               reproducible and the just-landed whisker fix is visible at a
               glance. DONE: examples/Compute/error-bars.json (next to its
               vector-fed sibling oscilloscope.json; that dir is tracked in git
               but, like Clocks/crypto/ meshtastic, intentionally not in
               examples/Makefile.am, so it ships in the repo not the install).
               Three startup-announcing Knobs (from=0, to=8pi, N=512) drive a
               Ramp -> Expression -> PnPlot chain, so the plot fills on load
               with no fiddling. The deterministic jitter is folded into the
               Expression itself -- "sin(value) + 0.18 * sin(value * 23)" --
               since there is no noise source node and Expression has no rand():
               the low-frequency sin gives the bucket-to-bucket trend (green
               rising / red falling boxes) and the high-frequency term gives
               genuine within-bucket spread (fat sd box + min/max whiskers).
